# 🟠 Level 3 – Advanced Concepts

# Why Browsers Execute It

Browsers trust code delivered by websites.

If a vulnerable application includes untrusted content without proper protection,

the browser may interpret it as active webpage content.

---

# Session Cookies

After login:

```
User

↓

Server

↓

Session Created

↓

Browser Stores Session Cookie
```

If session cookies are not properly protected, attackers may try to misuse them through XSS.

Modern applications reduce this risk using:

* HttpOnly cookies
* Secure cookies
* SameSite cookies
* Content Security Policy

---

# Content Security Policy (CSP)

CSP is a browser security feature.

It tells browsers:

> Which sources of scripts, images, and other resources are allowed.

Benefits:

✔ Reduces many XSS risks

✔ Blocks unauthorized scripts

✔ Limits resource loading

---

# Secure Cookie Flags

## HttpOnly

JavaScript cannot directly access the cookie.

Helps reduce certain XSS risks.

---

## Secure

Cookie is sent only over HTTPS.

---

## SameSite

Helps reduce certain cross-site request risks.

---

# 🌍 Real-World Example

A discussion forum allows users to post comments.

If comments are displayed without proper validation and output encoding,

other visitors may be affected by malicious content.

Proper output encoding and input validation prevent this.

---

# How Developers Prevent XSS

## 1. Output Encoding

Display user input as **text**, not executable content.

This is one of the most effective defenses.

---

## 2. Input Validation

Accept only expected input.

Example:

Age:

✔ Numbers only

Name:

✔ Letters

---

## 3. Content Security Policy

Restrict where scripts can be loaded from.

---

## 4. HttpOnly Cookies

Prevent JavaScript from directly reading session cookies.

---

## 5. Secure Frameworks

Modern frameworks provide built-in protections when used correctly.

Examples:

* React
* Angular
* Vue

Developers should still avoid bypassing built-in escaping features.

---

# Common Beginner Mistakes

❌ Displaying user input without encoding.

❌ Disabling browser security protections.

❌ Allowing unrestricted HTML where it isn't necessary.

❌ Ignoring Content Security Policy.

